<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ptolemy XII Auletes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 117–<a href="/years/51-bce">51 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Ptolemy XII</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ptolemy XII Auletes was king of Ptolemaic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> from 80 to 58 BCE and again from 55 to 51 BCE, and father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleopatra-vii">Cleopatra VII</a>. Dependent on Roman goodwill, he paid huge sums to Roman politicians for recognition, was driven out by the Alexandrians in 58 BCE, and was restored in 55 BCE by the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aulus-gabinius">Aulus Gabinius</a>, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ptolemy-xii-auletes.jpg" alt="Ptolemy XII Auletes" width="320" height="485" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marie-Lan Nguyen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ptolemy_XII_Auletes_Louvre_Ma3449.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ptolemy XII was the oldest son of Ptolemy IX, by a mother whose identity is uncertain. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a> and other ancient sources call him illegitimate, and some scholars propose that his mother was a concubine. His grandmother Cleopatra III sent him and his brother, later Ptolemy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, to the island of Kos in 103 BCE.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI of Pontus</a> seems to have captured them in 88 BCE, and held them as hostages until 80 BCE. After the killing of his cousin Ptolemy XI in 80 BCE, Ptolemy XII was recalled and proclaimed king in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> in April of that year. He was known as Auletes, meaning the flautist, for playing the flute at Dionysian festivals.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Ptolemy X had left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in his will if he had no surviving heirs, so Ptolemy XII followed a respectful policy towards Rome. In 65 BCE the censor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-licinius-crassus">Marcus Licinius Crassus</a> proposed to annex Egypt, but Quintus Lutatius Catulus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a> opposed it. Ptolemy began to bribe Roman politicians, funding this by raising taxes and by loans from Roman bankers such as Gaius Rabirius Postumus.</p><p>In 60 BCE he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, where he paid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> and Caesar six thousand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a>, equal to the total annual revenue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. The Senate recognised him as king, and in 59 BCE Caesar passed a law that named him a friend and ally of the Roman people.</p><p>In 58 BCE Rome took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, and his brother committed suicide. Ptolemy took no action, and the court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> forced him to leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. His daughter Berenice IV took the throne. He went to Rome, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> housed him. In 55 BCE he paid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aulus-gabinius">Aulus Gabinius</a> 10,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a> to invade Egypt, and Gabinius defeated the frontier forces and took the palace. Ptolemy had Berenice and her supporters executed, and ruled with the help of around two thousand Roman soldiers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, the Gabiniani. He made Rabirius his finance minister to put popular anger at the taxes on a Roman, and later had to imprison him to protect his life.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ptolemy XII died before 22 March 51 BCE, after falling ill. His daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleopatra-vii">Cleopatra VII</a> had been named his regent. In his will he made her and her brother Ptolemy XIII joint rulers and the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> the executors, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> approved the will. Ancient and modern writers portray him as weak and self-indulgent.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> calls him the worst of the later Ptolemies and says he took such pride in playing the flute that he held contests in the royal palace. Mary Siani-Davies writes that his aim throughout was to keep the throne for his heirs, at the cost of lands, wealth and, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a>, the dignity of kingship.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ptolemy XII Auletes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rome steadily extended its influence over Egyptian affairs: in 168 BCE a Roman envoy forced Antiochus IV to abandon his invasion of Egypt, and in 55 BCE Roman troops restored <b>Ptolemy XII</b> to his throne.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ptolemaic-empire">Ptolemaic Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35,33&amp;year=-75" aria-label="Cyprus on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5386 898 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ptolemy XII Auletes"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5836 1168h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5836 1168h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35,33&amp;year=-75">What was here in 75 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
